{"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"tokens":[{"text":"final","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"class","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"FrameTap","kind":"identifier"}],"platforms":["iOS","iPadOS","Mac Catalyst"],"languages":["swift"]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"kind":"content","content":[{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Overview","anchor":"overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A source hands each frame over after it has handed it to its presenter, and the tap only stores"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"a reference and calls its receiver, so it never holds the picture up. The receiver must return"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"at once; a recording’s queues its frame and drops it if the writer is still busy.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"A tap that keeps the newest frame holds one of the source’s buffers for as long as it is the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"newest. The helper’s stream keeps it, since a still Mac sends no new frames; the capture card","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"does not, since its frames come from a small pool that the output needs back, and a screenshot","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"takes the card’s next frame instead, a frame time away."}]},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The receiver is called under the tap’s lock, so frames reach it in the order of their arrival"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"times, the newest frame handed over on attach included."}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"patch":0,"minor":3},"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/headwirecapture\/frametap"]}],"topicSections":[{"title":"Initializers","identifiers":["doc:\/\/headwirecapture.HeadwireCapture\/documentation\/HeadwireCapture\/FrameTap\/init(keepsLatest:)"],"anchor